## 3. The Backdrop: Sector and Economy

### 3.1 Technology sector

In Week 1, I identified the main drivers of technology stocks: the AI spending cycle, interest rates, the concentration of index performance in a few large companies, and policy and supply risks. October 2026 data fits that picture. The S&P 500 and Nasdaq reached record highs in early October, with the Nasdaq Composite up 17.7% for the year, and market commentary put the AI chip group at the centre of the rally. At the same time the Dow was still about 5% below its August record in early October, and a major bank ETF was about 12% below its mid-August high, so leadership is narrow.

### 3.2 The economy (Week 5)

The economy is growing steadily but with warning lights. US GDP rose 2.2% in the second quarter, September payrolls rose only 29,000 and inflation reached 3.7% as gasoline prices rose 34% over the year. The Federal Reserve raised rates on 16 September to 3.75% to 4.00%, and the 10-year Treasury yield reached about 5.24% on 9 October, the highest in more than two decades. My base case is range-bound markets (about 50%), with a soft landing (about 25%) and a stagflation scare (about 25%) as the alternatives.

The main message for stock selection is that valuation matters more than usual. When a risk-free asset pays more than 5%, the price paid for each dollar of profit has less room to be generous. That favours businesses valued reasonably against their earnings, which is one reason the two recommendations differ.

