### 4.5 Conclusion on Microsoft: Hold

The business deserves a place in a long-term portfolio. The price today does not give a margin of safety. If you own it, keep it, and if it has grown past about 5% of the portfolio, trim it back. If you do not own it, I would not buy at this level. I would build a position in stages on a pullback: a first third in the $480 to $505 zone around the 50-day averages, and a second third in the $435 to $456 zone around the 200-day averages. I would reassess after the 28 October results, where capital spending guidance matters most.

## 5. Company Two: JPMorgan Chase

### 5.1 Financial review

JPMorgan reported third-quarter results on 13 October 2026. Banks are different from technology companies. Revenue comes mostly from net interest income (the gap between what it earns on loans and pays on deposits) and from fee and trading businesses, and the key risks are credit losses and capital strength.

| Item | Q3 2026 | Comment |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Net income | $15.3 billion, up 6% | $15.8 billion excluding a $658 million securities loss |
| Diluted EPS | $5.57, up 10% (from $5.07) | $5.75 excluding the loss; estimates were roughly $5.9 |
| Net revenue | $50.9 billion, up 8% | Net interest income $27.1 billion, up 13%; fee revenue $23.8 billion, up 3% |
| Expenses | $27.3 billion, up 12% | Hiring, wages and revenue-linked pay |
| Credit costs | $2.7 billion | $2.2 billion net charge-offs and a $539 million reserve build |
| Return on tangible equity | 20% (21% excluding the loss) | Very high for a bank |
| Book value / tangible book value per share | $132.30 / $112.64 | Up 6% and 7% over the year |
| Capital | CET1 ratio 13.9% | Well above regulatory minimums |
| Shareholder returns | Dividend $1.65 per share; $664 million net buybacks | Buybacks reduced to prioritise lending growth |
| Outlook | Fourth-quarter net interest income about $28 billion | Management more selective on credit |

*Table 6. JPMorgan third-quarter 2026 highlights. Source: JPMorgan Chase earnings release (Form 8-K) and earnings call.*

Earnings were good but not perfect: earnings per share missed expectations, the first miss since late 2023, because of an investment securities loss. Underneath, the results were strong. The Corporate and Investment Bank earned $8.2 billion (up 20%), consumer banking $5.7 billion (up 13%) and asset and wealth management $1.8 billion (up 11%). The weaker points are costs, which are growing faster than revenue, and credit, where management said it is becoming more selective and is watching risks from high interest rates, AI-related financing and market volatility. Morningstar noted that markets revenue, though 15% higher than a year ago, fell 15% from the second quarter, so the exceptional pace of the first half is fading.

### 5.2 Valuation

Banks are usually valued against tangible book value. A common approach ties the right multiple to profitability: fair price to tangible book equals (return on tangible equity minus growth) divided by (cost of equity minus growth). I assumed growth of 4%, a return on tangible equity between 17% and 21%, and a cost of equity between 9% and 11%. JPMorgan's tangible book value per share is $112.64.

| Cost of equity \ ROTCE | 17% | 18% | 19% | 20% | 21% |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 9% | $293 | $315 | $338 | $360 | $383 |
| 10% | $244 | $263 | $282 | $300 | $319 |
| 11% | $209 | $225 | $241 | $257 | $274 |

*Table 7. Value per share from tangible book value, growth of 4%.*

My central case uses a 20% return (the reported level), a 9.5% cost of equity and 4% growth. That gives a multiple of 2.91 times tangible book, or about $328 a share. The grid shows how sensitive this is: the price of $336 needs roughly a 9% cost of equity with a 19% return, or a 20% return with a cost of equity around 9.3%. A cross-check on earnings gives a similar answer. At 12 to 16 times the 2026 estimate of $24.13 (a consensus figure from before the results), the range is $290 to $386, and the shares trade at 13.9 times. Morningstar's fair value is $330 and the pre-earnings consensus target is $372. The shares also yield about 2.0% in dividends.

Put together, JPMorgan looks fairly valued: not cheap, but priced for continued strong profitability, not for perfection.

### 5.3 Technical picture

JPMorgan has been in an ascending channel since the April 2025 low of $202.16. It hit a record of $366.50 on 13 August, then pulled back about 11% to $325.87 on 1 October, stalling at the 200-day average (about $322 to $325) and a 61.8% Fibonacci retracement. RSI of about 60 and a slightly positive MACD are neutral to firm. Short-term averages ($337 to $352) still sit above the price. A close above $351.55 would confirm that buyers are back; a close below about $321 would break the channel and point to $315.70.

