## 7. Risk Assessment for the Recommendations

Week 4 showed that the risk in a portfolio comes less from any single company than from how much of the portfolio moves together. Microsoft and JPMorgan are different kinds of businesses but both would fall in a broad stock market sell-off, and both have a link to interest rates. The table shows how each would be expected to behave in the three scenarios from Week 5.

| Scenario (Week 5) | Microsoft | JPMorgan | What I would do |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Base case (about 50%): range-bound market | Flat to slightly lower; valuation caps gains | Steady; earnings support the price | Follow the staged plan in Table 11 |
| Soft landing (about 25%): energy falls, Fed stops | Positive; lower yields help a growth stock | Neutral to positive; credit stays benign | Let the plan run; Microsoft may not pull back, so accept a smaller position |
| Stagflation scare (about 25%): oil and inflation rise | Negative; the most exposed to higher yields | Negative on credit worries, partly offset by interest income | Pause new buying, hold the defensive sleeves of the portfolio |

*Table 10. Behaviour by scenario. These are qualitative judgements.*

Using the Week 4 results as a guide, a concentrated technology portfolio lost about 27% in the 2022 rate-shock scenario against about 16% for the diversified one, and about 32% in my hypothetical AI-unwind scenario against about 16%. That is why neither company should be a large part of the portfolio, however good it looks.

